scopy: copy a file for another user, controlled by an access list

WHAT IT DOES
scopy lets the owner of a file share it with chosen users without changing normal UNIX file permissions.
The owner makes a list of allowed user names in a file called "<file>.acl". Anyone the list names can run
the owner's copy of scopy to copy the file. The program is meant to run with its SetUID bit set, so it
runs with the owner's rights.

The .acl file has one line per allowed user: a user name, a space, and one letter (for example "bob r").

EXAMPLE
Alice wants to share her schedule with Bob. She:
  1. puts a copy of scopy in her public directory, /home/alice/public/scopy,
  2. makes sure Bob can run it, and sets its SetUID bit:  chmod u+s scopy
  3. creates /home/alice/daily_schedule.txt.acl containing the line:  bob r

Bob then copies the file with:
  bob@wopr $ /home/alice/public/scopy /home/alice/daily_schedule.txt alices_schedule.txt

BUILD
  make          compiles scopy
  make clean    removes scopy and the object files
(scopy uses sendfile() from <sys/sendfile.h>, so it builds on Linux.)

FILES
  scopy.c       the program (main)
  functions.h   helper functions: privilege switching, symlink and ownership checks
  Makefile      build rules
  file.txt, file.txt.acl   a sample file and its access list

CHECKS THE PROGRAM MAKES
  - two arguments are given (source and destination)
  - the source file and its .acl file are regular files with a single hard link (no symlinks)
  - the .acl file exists and is owned by the same user as scopy; the source belongs to scopy's owner
  - the user running scopy (looked up from the real user id) appears in the .acl file
  - errors opening, reading, or writing a file are reported instead of ignored

KNOWN LIMITS (not changed)
  - The program drops its extra privileges at the start and never restores them (do_setuid() is never
    called), so it can only read files the calling user could already read.
  - The letter after each name in the .acl file is read but not used: every listed user is allowed.
  - The checks and the later open() are separate steps, so a file could be swapped in between them.
  - At most 200 lines of the .acl file are read.
